        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Rebounders</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these rebounders. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages,
            manuals, retailer listings, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on
            this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the choices a buyer actually faces — bungee or spring, frame size,
            weight rating, handlebar and price — and looked for a rebounder on sale on 19 October
            2026 that represents each well. JumpSport's and Leaps & Rebounds' figures were checked on
            their own stores against second retailers (Northern Fitness, School Specialty, Best Buy
            Canada and Walmart listings) and Amazon listing data. bellicon's were checked against its
            own US site, Free People's listing and Amazon listing data. BCAN's were checked between
            its Amazon listing and Best Buy's listing, and against an independent owner-review
            summary; Marcy's between its own store, its manual and its Amazon listing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand was searched in the CPSC recall database by recall title, manufacturer and
            recall description, along with the product type. Two recalls of fitness mini-trampolines
            came up, both about the frame snapping shut during folding or assembly; they are set out
            in <a href="#recalls" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the recalls section</a>. JumpSport
            is the brand involved in the more recent one, in 2019. It covered older half-folding
            models and was remedied with new instructions and warning labels rather than a refund;
            the JumpSport models carded here are non-folding, arched-leg designs. We found no recalls
            for bellicon, Leaps & Rebounds, BCAN, Marcy, Needak or Pure Fun. Other trampoline recalls
            in the database involve backyard and children's trampolines, which are outside this
            guide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we left out, and why.</strong> Needak's Soft-Bounce, a well-regarded
            spring rebounder made in Nebraska, was listed on Needak's own store at $399.99 with a
            40-inch frame, 36 springs and a 300 lb rating, but the Amazon listings we found for it
            showed as unavailable, so it is mentioned here rather than carded. We left out JumpSport's
            folding 44-inch model sold on Amazon: it carries the name of one of the 2019 recalled
            models, and we could not confirm how the current version's hinge differs. Pure Fun's
            exercise trampolines are widely sold, but we could not open either the maker's site or a
            retailer listing on the day, so we had nothing to cross-check. And we did not include
            children's or toddler trampolines; a fitness rebounder is adult exercise equipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bungee-vs-springs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Bungee vs Springs: Which Suspension</h2>
<p>
            The mat is held to the frame either by elastic bungee cords (or bungee rings) or by
            steel coil springs. This is the biggest decision, because it sets the feel, the noise and
            the maintenance.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bungee cords</h3>
          <p>
            Bungees stretch further, so the mat sinks deeper and the bounce feels softer and slower
            at the bottom. Makers of bungee rebounders — bellicon, JumpSport and Leaps & Rebounds on
            this page — describe that as gentler on the joints and quieter, and bungees do not
            squeak the way a dry spring can. Many bungee rebounders let you change the firmness:
            bellicon supplies bungees in five strengths chosen by body weight, and JumpSport's PRO
            cords can be set from firm to extra firm. The trade-off is lifespan. Rubber cords
            gradually lose tension and are a wear part, and replacements are an ongoing cost; a JumpSport
            replacement set of 36 cords was listed at $119 to $139 on 19 October.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Steel springs</h3>
          <p>
            Springs give a firmer, faster, more "trampoline-like" rebound with less sink. They are
            cheap to make, which is why most budget rebounders, including the BCAN and Marcy models
            here, use them. A well-made spring system can last a long time — Needak, which makes
            spring rebounders, argues that springs outlast bungees — but cheap springs and the rings
            that hold them are a common failure point, and springs can squeak and need occasional
            attention. Look for a cover over the springs so feet cannot slip between them.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which to choose</h3>
          <p>
            Choose bungees if you want the softest landing, a quiet bounce in a shared home, or
            adjustable firmness, and accept paying more and replacing cords eventually. Choose
            springs if your budget is under about $150, you like a firmer bounce, or you weigh more
            than a soft bungee setup is rated for. Either way, the firmness has to suit your weight:
            a bounce that bottoms out on the floor is uncomfortable and unsafe, and one that is too
            stiff feels harsh.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-and-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Frame Size, Mat Size and Weight Limits</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Frame diameter</strong> is the figure in the name — 39, 40, 44, 48 or 49 inches
            on this page. The usable jumping mat is much smaller, because the suspension and its
            cover take up the outer ring. Retailer listings put JumpSport's 39-inch models at a mat
            of about 32.5 to 33 inches, and Marcy lists a 27 by 27 inch jumping surface on its
            40-inch square model. A small mat keeps you centred, which some makers argue gives a
            more even bounce; a larger one gives room for wider stances, jacks and side-to-side
            moves and suits taller users.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a rough guide: a 39- to 40-inch frame suits gentle bouncing, jogging in place and
            small spaces; a 44-inch frame is the middle ground most makers describe as their
            best-seller; a 48- or 49-inch frame suits taller people, more lateral movement and
            anyone who wants floor exercises on the mat. Measure the floor and add room to step on
            and off safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight ratings</strong> on this page run from 250 lb (JumpSport ESSENTIAL, Marcy)
            through 270 lb (Leaps & Rebounds 48-inch), 300 lb (JumpSport PRO) and 330 lb (BCAN 40-inch)
            to bellicon's bungee system, which its US listings rate up to 440 lb with its strongest
            bungees. These are maker maximums, not targets. On a rebounder, your weight is
            multiplied by the bounce, so a rating is about the frame, legs and suspension coping with
            repeated dynamic loads. Leave a margin, buy for the heaviest user, and on adjustable or
            selectable bungee systems choose the strength for your weight, not the softest one. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains how to read ratings like these, and our
            <a href="/what-happens-if-you-exceed-treadmill-weight-limit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to exceeding a weight limit</a>
            covers what goes wrong on machines when people do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Legs.</strong> Arched legs, as on the JumpSport models, angle outward to widen
            the base and resist tipping. Screw-on legs make a rebounder easier to store flat or
            upright. Folding frames save the most space but involve a hinge, which is where both
            mini-trampoline recalls in the CPSC database came from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handle-bar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Do You Need a Handlebar?</h2>
<p>
            A handlebar, stability bar or T-bar is a frame-mounted rail you can hold while bouncing.
            It is the single most useful accessory for some buyers and an unnecessary one for others.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Buy one</strong> if you are new to exercise, older, have any balance
              concern, are returning to activity, or want to do faster footwork and sprints with
              something to steady you. A bar also makes getting on and off easier.</li>
            <li><strong>Skip it</strong> if your balance is good and you want free arm movement for
              jacks, punches or weights; a bar restricts the space in front of you.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the height range.</strong> BCAN lists its SP3 U-handle at 32 to 42
              inches; Leaps & Rebounds lists an adjustable bar for its rebounders. A bar should sit
              around hip to waist height while you stand on the mat.</li>
            <li><strong>Check it is designed for that frame.</strong> A bar from another brand, or
              for a different leg style, may not attach securely. JumpSport sells different bars for
              straight-leg and arched-leg models.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Of the picks, the BCAN and Marcy come with a handle; bellicon offers a T-bar on its 44-
            and 49-inch models; Leaps & Rebounds sells a bundle with a stability bar ($313 for the
            40-inch on 19 October); and JumpSport sells bars separately and a 39-inch PRO bundle
            with one ($508). If balance is your main concern rather than cardio, our
            <a href="/best-balance-boards/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">balance board guide</a>
            covers a different kind of training.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ceiling-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Ceiling Height, Space and Floors</h2>
<p>
            Rebounding is mostly small bounces with your feet staying close to the mat, but you
            still need overhead room. No maker we checked publishes a single required ceiling
            height for its fitness rebounders, so here is the arithmetic, using stated inputs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Add four things: the height of the mat off the floor, your height, how far you leave the
            mat on your biggest bounce, and a safety margin. Leaps & Rebounds' rebounders are listed
            at about 13 inches tall and Needak's legs at 10 inches, so call the mat 10 to 13 inches.
            For a 5'10" (70-inch) person on a 12-inch mat, leaving the mat by 6 inches, with 6 inches
            of margin, that is 12 + 70 + 6 + 6 = 94 inches — just under a standard 8-foot (96-inch)
            ceiling. A 6'2" person bouncing a foot high needs 12 + 74 + 12 + 6 = 104 inches, or
            nearly 9 feet. Taller users in an 8-foot room should keep bounces low or rebound
            somewhere with more height. Remember ceiling fans, light fittings and sloped ceilings,
            and check the doorway frame if you set up near one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor space.</strong> Allow clear floor around the frame so a misstep lands on
            the floor, not on furniture: a few feet on every side is sensible, more in front if you
            step off forward. Keep it away from walls, glass and stairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floors and noise.</strong> A rebounder is far quieter for the room below than
            jumping on the floor, because the mat absorbs the landing, but the legs still pass a
            rhythmic thump into the floor, and a squeaky spring is audible through walls. In an
            upstairs room or apartment, bungees are generally the quieter choice, and a dense rubber
            mat under the legs protects the floor and reduces transmitted noise; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            covers options. The
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment treadmill guide</a>
            explains the difference between airborne and structure-borne noise, which applies
            here too. On carpet, make sure all the legs sit level and firm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Rebounder Safety and the Recalls</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC database holds two recalls of fitness mini-trampolines, and they describe the
            same hazard: the frame springing shut on the user.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Recalled product</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Hazard and reports</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sold</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">JumpSport half-fold fitness trampolines, models 230f, 350f (39 in.), 550f Pro and 550fi (44 in.), recalled February 2019; about 11,300 in the US</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding frame can forcefully hit the user; nine injury reports including cuts, bruises and dental and facial injuries</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">January 2011 to November 2018, at Amazon, Costco, JumpSport and others, $250 to $500</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stamina InMotion 36-inch mini-trampolines, model numbers containing 35-1625 (not 35-1625C or 35-1625CW), recalled April 2006; about 668,000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">During solo assembly the outer rail can snap back to the folded position; 13 injury reports including concussions, facial bone fractures and chipped teeth</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">August 2000 to March 2006, at Walmart and others, about $20</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Both remedies were new instructions and warnings rather than refunds. JumpSport told
            owners to stop using the recalled models until they had downloaded the new manual and
            warning materials, and not to fold a set-up trampoline before reading them; the model
            number is on a label on one leg. Stamina told owners not to assemble or disassemble
            the trampolines until they had the revised instructions. If you own or are buying a used
            folding rebounder from either brand, check the model number against the notice on
            cpsc.gov or saferproducts.gov.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The lesson for any rebounder:</strong> a folding frame stores energy, and the
            dangerous moment is folding, unfolding or assembling, not bouncing. Follow the manual
            exactly, keep your face and hands out of the line of the frame, never fold one alone if
            the manual says not to, and do not improvise with a hinge that feels stiff. BCAN's SP3
            folds only after you remove the handle and legs, which an independent owner-review
            summary calls a serious task; that is slower, but it is not a spring-loaded snap.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Everyday safety habits:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Inspect before each session: cords or springs intact and evenly tensioned, cover in
              place, legs screwed tight, mat free of tears.</li>
            <li>Bounce in the centre, with small bounces, and step on and off rather than jumping
              off.</li>
            <li>Wear grip socks or supportive shoes as the maker recommends; never bounce in
              ordinary socks on a slick mat.</li>
            <li>One person at a time, within the weight rating.</li>
            <li>Keep children off adult rebounders unless the maker says otherwise; Marcy's manual
              says its trampoline is not intended for children under 12, and a 2026 recall of
              SEGMART toddler trampolines over strangulation-hazard accessory straps is a reminder
              that what is attached to a trampoline matters too.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="health-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Rebounding Does, and the Claims Around It</h2>
<p>
            Bouncing on a rebounder is exercise: it raises your heart rate, works the legs and trunk,
            and challenges balance, with softer landings than jumping on a hard floor. How hard it is
            depends almost entirely on you — gentle "health bounces" with your feet barely leaving
            the mat are light activity, while sprinting in place or jumping jacks for minutes at a
            time is hard work. For the method behind energy estimates, see our explainer on
            <a href="/what-are-mets-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">METs</a>;
            we do not quote a calorie figure for rebounding, because the effort varies so widely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rebounder marketing goes further. Leaps & Rebounds says its bungee rebounder gives "70%
            less joint impact than treadmill running" and "activates your lymphatic system";
            bellicon's FAQ says rebounding gets the lymphatic system moving "more effectively than
            walking or running" and lists weight loss among its benefits; Marcy's page says its
            trampoline results in increased bone and muscle density and weight loss.
            <strong>These are the sellers' claims.</strong> We could not find the source of the 70%
            figure on Leaps & Rebounds' page, and lymphatic and bone-density effects are medical
            questions we are not in a position to assess.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many sellers cite a 1980 study funded by NASA that compared jumping on a trampoline with
            running on a treadmill. It was a small laboratory study in young men that measured body
            acceleration and oxygen uptake; it did not test weight loss, lymphatic flow or bone
            density, and it used a full-size trampoline rather than a modern fitness rebounder.
            Treat claims that lean on it with care.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Who should ask first.</strong> bellicon's own FAQ says pregnant women, people
            with severe balance disorders, acute injuries or uncontrolled cardiovascular conditions,
            and anyone above their model's weight limit should consult a doctor before starting, and
            JumpSport's site tells everyone to consult a physician before starting a new exercise
            program. Repeated bouncing also loads the pelvic floor, ankles and knees, so people who
            are postpartum or have pelvic floor symptoms, osteoporosis, or ankle, knee or back
            problems should ask a qualified professional what is appropriate. That is general
            guidance, not medical advice. If joint comfort is the main reason you are looking, our
            guide to whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are bad for your knees</a>
            covers the impact question in general terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want zero impact</strong>, a rebounder is low impact, not no impact. An
            exercise bike, elliptical or rowing machine keeps your feet or seat in contact
            throughout; see our
            <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">exercise bike</a>,
            <a href="/best-elliptical-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">elliptical</a>
            and <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rowing machine</a>
            guides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want to train for running or walking</strong>, a treadmill or <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a>
            trains the movement itself. Our
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio equipment guide</a>
            helps choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want cheap, small-space cardio with no ceiling concern</strong>, a jump
            rope costs far less and packs into a drawer, though it is higher impact; our
            <a href="/best-jump-ropes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">jump rope guide</a>
            compares them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you were considering a vibration plate</strong> for low-effort sessions, a
            rebounder is more clearly exercise; our
            <a href="/best-vibration-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vibration plate guide</a>
            explains what plates do and do not do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a very low ceiling or are tall</strong>, run the arithmetic above
            before buying. A rebounder you cannot bounce on properly will end up against a wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best rebounder for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the JumpSport 39-inch PRO (sold as the 350 PRO): 36 adjustable bungee cords, stable arched legs and a 300 lb rating, at about $410 to $480. bellicon is the premium choice with bungees matched to your weight, Leaps & Rebounds is the value bungee pick, and BCAN or Marcy rebounders with handles suit tight budgets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are bungee or spring rebounders better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better for everyone. Bungee rebounders give a softer, deeper, quieter bounce and often adjustable firmness, but cost more and their cords wear and need replacing. Spring rebounders are cheaper and firmer and can last well if well made, but cheap springs can squeak or fail. Choose bungees for comfort and quiet, springs for budget and a firmer bounce.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size rebounder should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 39- to 40-inch frame suits gentle bouncing, jogging in place and small rooms. A 44-inch frame is the versatile middle size. A 48- or 49-inch frame suits taller users, more side-to-side movement and floor exercises on the mat. Remember the usable mat is several inches smaller than the frame diameter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high does a ceiling need to be for a rebounder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add the mat height (about 10 to 13 inches), your height, how high you bounce and a margin. A 5'10" person on a 12-inch mat bouncing 6 inches, with 6 inches spare, needs about 94 inches, just under an 8-foot ceiling. A 6'2" person bouncing a foot needs nearly 9 feet. Taller users in low rooms should keep bounces small.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rebounding good for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rebounding is exercise, and like any exercise it uses energy, but how much depends on how hard you work: gentle bounces are light activity, sprints and jacks are hard. Claims of special fat-burning, lymphatic or bone benefits are the sellers'. For weight management, the activity you do most consistently, plus diet, matters most.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are mini trampolines safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Used as directed, within the weight limit and with small, centred bounces, fitness rebounders are a common form of home exercise. The recalls of fitness mini-trampolines involved frames that snapped shut during folding or assembly, so follow the manual exactly at those moments. Check cords or springs before each session, and keep children off adult rebounders.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Rebounder</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and frustrating to discover afterwards.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the weight rating, or the bungee strength</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A rebounder carries your weight multiplied by every bounce. Buy one rated comfortably above the heaviest user, and on a bellicon or adjustable JumpSport choose or set the bungee strength for your weight rather than the softest option. A mat that bottoms out near the floor is uncomfortable and unsafe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">People measure the floor and forget the room's height. Add the mat height, your height, your bounce and a margin before ordering. In a standard 8-foot room, a tall person bouncing high can reach the ceiling, a light fitting or a fan, so check, or plan to keep bounces small.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Folding it carelessly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both CPSC recalls of fitness mini-trampolines involved frames snapping shut during folding or assembly, with facial and dental injuries. Read the folding instructions before the first fold, keep your face and hands out of the frame's path, and check any used folding rebounder's model number against the recall notices.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a handle-less model when you need support</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you are new to exercise, older, have any balance concern or are returning from injury, a handlebar is not optional. Adding one later costs more than buying a bundle, and bars from other brands may not fit. If balance is a real concern, talk to a qualified professional before starting.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

